    "result_text": "The full suite is green. There's a single test class in the project \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` \u2014 and both tests pass, with all main sources (including the MapStruct-generated mapper) compiling cleanly.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe locality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city table.\n\n**`LocalityLookup.java`** \u2014 added a two-arg `regionFor(city, postcode)`:\n- `regionForPostcode` maps a 4-digit postcode to its region via the range table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099), returning `null` when the postcode is absent, non-4-digit, or in no known range.\n- When the postcode resolves a region it wins; otherwise it falls back to the existing city-to-region lookup. The original single-arg `regionFor(city)` is retained (still used by `ValidateOwnerPostcode`).\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping expression now passes both `owner.getCity()` and `owner.getPostcode()`.\n\nThis returns the same region for known cities (e.g. Sydney @ 2000 \u2192 NSW) but disambiguates same-named cities via postcode \u2014 a VIC-range postcode (3000) on an otherwise-unknown city now yields VIC, exactly as the new acceptance tests assert.",
